  "id": "Compound::DB08820 - Gene::5243",
  "verbalization": "Ivacaftor is linked to Abcb1 through a series of molecular interactions involving cytochrome P450 enzymes. The evidence suggests that Ivacaftor, as a member of the Cytochrome P450 3a4 family, inhibits enzymes that ultimately bind to Abcb1. Specifically, Fluconazole, which is included in the Cytochrome P450 3a4 Inhibitors group, binds to Abcb1. This indicates a potential mechanism by which Ivacaftor may influence Abcb1 activity, possibly through indirect pathways involving enzyme inhibition. The shared lowest common ancestor of Ivacaftor and Fluconazole, both classified as Organic Hydroxy Compounds, further supports a common molecular structure that could mediate these interactions. The evidence highlights a dependency on cytochrome P450 enzymes to modulate Abcb1 function, suggesting a broader role for these enzymes in drug metabolism and transport.",
